  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent Poisson random variables is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=-4\\theta +8\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡24\n$$\n\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}$ from this sample?",
  "worked_solution": "In order to find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta ,$ we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta .$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}l}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(-4\\theta +8\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡24) \\\\  & =-4+\\frac{8}{\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4+\\frac{8}{\\theta } & =0 \\\\ \\frac{-4\\theta +8}{\\theta } & =0 \\\\ -4\\theta +8 & =0 \\\\ \\theta  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta $ is $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}=2.$",
